    "display_name": "Movie Recommendations",
    "long_instructions": "You’re building a program that recommends movies for users to watch. To let the user know how long it will take to watch your movie recommendations, this program has a `get_average_length` function. The function accepts two movie lengths and returns their average length.\r\n\r\n**Do This**\r\n\r\n1. Update the `get_average_length` function to return the average movie length. Remember that you can calculate the average of two numbers by adding them together and then dividing the result by 2.\r\n2. Call the `get_average_length` function to get the average movie length. Save the result in a new variable.\r\n3. Use your new variable to print a message that tells the user the average length.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nFor functions that return a value, you can save the value in a variable when you call the function. For example, if a function called `get_length` returned a value, we could save it in a new variable called `length` with a line like this:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nlength = get_length()\r\n```\r\n\r\nPython follows the order of operations in math (multiplication and division happen before addition and subtraction), so if you want to control the order of operations, you can use parentheses. For example, this expression will be calculated as 14:\r\n\r\n```python\r\n2 + 3 * 4\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd this expression will be calculated as 20:\r\n\r\n```python\r\n(2 + 3) * 4\r\n```",
